$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/ai-foundations/ai-assistant-setup

::ai-assistant-setup o1 - Three inputs::Lee opens an assistant at the project folder, shares config.py and types "Make the request timeout configurable." The project has no rules file. Which of the assistant's inputs is missing? {
=Standing instructions #Correct. With no project rules file, the assistant gets shared context (config.py) and a prompt, but no standing instructions.
~Shared context #Not quite. Lee shared config.py, so the assistant can see that file; the rules file is what is missing.
~The prompt #Not quite. Lee typed a request, and that request is the prompt; the missing input is the standing instructions.
~Nothing is missing, because the assistant can also read the team's chat history #Not quite. An assistant works only from standing instructions, shared context and your prompt; it has no other view of your project.
}

::ai-assistant-setup o2 - Missing context::You ask an assistant "Add a line that writes errors to our log file." You share no files and no rules, and your project keeps its log in a folder the assistant has never seen. What is the most likely result? {
=A line that writes to a plausible but guessed file name, which you still need to check #Correct. A missing input gets filled with something plausible, and whatever comes back is a proposal until you check it.
~A line that writes to your project's real log file, because assistants know common setups #Not quite. The assistant cannot see your project beyond its inputs, so it cannot know your real log location; it guesses.
~The same answer it would give if you had shared the project's files #Not quite. Shared context changes what the assistant can see; without it, the answer rests on guesses.
~No answer at all until you share a file #Not quite. The usual behavior taught here is that the assistant fills the gap with a plausible guess rather than stopping.
}

::ai-assistant-setup o3 - Workspace scope::Kim is fixing a bug in the billing project at /home/kim/work/billing. The fix changes src/invoice.py and its test in tests/test_invoice.py. Where should Kim open the assistant's workspace? {
=/home/kim/work/billing, the project folder for the task #Correct. Opening the project folder brings the files the change depends on into view without unrelated or private files.
~/home/kim, so the assistant can see everything Kim might need #Not quite. Too wide a scope brings private and unrelated files into view.
~/home/kim/work/billing/src/invoice.py only, to keep the assistant focused #Not quite. Too narrow a scope loses files the change depends on, such as the test file.
~/ (the whole computer), so nothing is ever missing #Not quite. The whole computer is far wider than the task and exposes private and unrelated files.
}
